Title: setup-claude-agent.sh greps .env for a key that no longer lives there
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T13:31Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/setup-claude-agent.sh:75`:

```sh
grep 'ANTHROPIC_API_KEY' "$HARNESS/.env" | sudo tee /etc/claude-agent/env >/dev/null
```

This assumes `~/.claude/.env` holds literal `VAR=value` credential assignments.
It has not since the KEYS mechanism landed — the file's own header states "NO
SECRET MATERIAL LIVES HERE. This file names keys; it never holds them."

So the script was already wrong before 0939 touched anything: its grep matched
the `KEYS=` line by substring and wrote the whole *selector* line into
`/etc/claude-agent/env`, where the service would read it as a credential
assignment named KEYS. Removing `anthropic` from KEYS (2026-09-16) changes the
failure mode from "writes the wrong thing" to "writes nothing"; it does not
introduce the defect.

The grep-a-comment hazard is real and was hit during that edit: a first pass at
documenting the removal spelled the variable out in a `.env` comment, which the
grep would have piped verbatim into the service env file. The `.env` comment now
says explicitly why the token must not appear there — but a guard would be
better than a warning nobody reads.

Not fixed in the 0939 PR: how the agent should obtain its key is a design
question (read the keystore directly? a helper invoked at need?), and it is the
same question as the cross-runtime portability of the secrets mechanism.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `setup-claude-agent.sh` obtains the key from the keystore, not by grepping `.env`
- [ ] It fails loudly when the key cannot be resolved, rather than writing an
      empty or malformed `/etc/claude-agent/env`
- [ ] A test covers the resolution path with a fake sentinel, never a real key
- [ ] No code path can write a `.env` comment line into a service env file
- [ ] `make check` passes
